// result_end

> Results

```
=== Interpolation search in uniform [1,3,...,19] ===
  key 13: found at index 6 (1 probes)
  key 1: found at index 0 (1 probes)
  key 19: found at index 9 (1 probes)
  key 8: not found (1 probes)
  key 20: not found (0 probes)

=== Skewed array [1..9,1000], searching 9 ===
  interpolation: found=true probes=9
  binary:        found=true probes=3

wrote interpolation_search.svg
```

On the uniform array interpolation search lands on 13 in a single probe \-\- the
interpolation formula is exact when the values are linear in the index. The skewed
array tells the other half of the story: the outlier 1000 warps the estimate so
badly that the search probes far more elements than binary search, illustrating
the \(O(n)\) worst case that is the price of trusting the data's distribution.


Geometrically, interpolation search draws the secant line (blue) between the two
endpoint values and reads off where it crosses the key level (grey): on uniform
data every point lies ~on~ that line, so the predicted index (gold) is exactly the
answer. The more the data bow away from the secant, the worse the guess \-\- and the
more probes the search needs.
